-- EXPECT-ERROR: which has side effects
-- Capabilities widening, Task 5 (F2 fix) -- `cap pure` (`check_pure_module`,
-- `typecheck.ml:6581`), the REJECT side that the F2 fix makes possible: a
-- `cap pure` module whose body calls the REAL effectful builtin `file_write`.
-- Pre-fix, `pure_banned` was a hand-maintained name list that spelled the
-- file builtin `write_file` (a name that DOES NOT EXIST) and MISSED the real
-- `file_write` -- so this program typechecked with exit 0 (UNSOUND; only
-- F1's body-scan HINT+WARNING fired, never `cap pure`'s own error). The fix
-- rebuilds `pure_banned` from `builtin_cap_table` (`typecheck.ml:999`, the
-- authoritative effect map), so every effectful builtin -- including
-- `file_write` (mapped `IO.FileWrite`) -- is now banned. The signature is
-- `Result(Unit, String)` because `file_write : String -> String ->
-- Result(Unit, r)` returns a `Result`, NOT `Unit`; a naive `: Unit`
-- signature would exit 1 for a TYPE mismatch, masking the cap violation.
-- Here the type is correct, so the ONLY rejection is `check_pure_module`'s
-- own: `` `write` in `mod LeakyPure` (declared `cap pure`) calls
-- `file_write`, which has side effects. `` (A WARNING-level Check 1b
-- body-scan diagnostic -- "requires `Cap(IO.FileWrite)`" -- also fires
-- alongside, per F1; it does not change the exit code and is not the
-- substring pinned here.) See core-march-types.md §2.8.11.
mod LeakyPure do
  needs IO.FileWrite
  cap pure

  fn write(path : String, contents : String) : Result(Unit, String) do
    file_write(path, contents)
  end
end
